WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.900
Hi, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:04.640
A geometry textbook has the following problem:

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.760
A right triangle has a hypotenuse equal to 10

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.560
and an altitude to the hypotenuse equal to 6.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.360
What is the area of the triangle?

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.320
Can you figure it out?

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.980
The story goes: A teacher asks this problem to a class,

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.920
and every single student quicky submitted a calculation.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.940
The teacher then said NONE of the calculations were correct.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.040
Can you figure out why?

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.720
Give this problem a try and when you're ready, keep watching the video for the solution.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.520
You're going to approach this problem by drawing a diagram.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.600
We have a right triangle

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.620
which has a hypotenuse of 10

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.440
and an altitude to the hypotenuse equal to 6.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.060
The area of a triangle is equal to its base times its height divided by 2.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.120
To apply this formula, it might help to rotate the triangle.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.680
The hypotenuse of 10 becomes the base,

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.140
and the altitude of 6 becomes the height.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.200
Substituting those numbers in the formula,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.560
we get an area equal to 30.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.640
But this is NOT the correct answer to this problem.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.120
The reason is the dimensions of the problem are not correct.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:20.800
Let's see why.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.680
Imagine we have a hypotenuse equal to 10.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.160
 

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.520
If the two end points of this line segment make up two points of the triangle,

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.340
we want to know: where can we draw a third point?

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.580
Where can the third vertex be to make a right triangle?

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.440
Now, not every point will make a right triangle.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.860
But some point will form a right triangle.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:48.080
So we want to figure out the locus of points where we can place the third vertex to make a right triangle.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:51.160
Here is how we can figure it out:

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.700
If the third vertex forms a 90-degree angle,

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.440
then it's going to subtend an arc that is 180 degrees.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:03.480
So we think about the hypotenuse as the diameter of a circle,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.820
then the third vertex can be anywhere else in the circle to form a right triangle.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.540
These are the only points thal will form a right triangle with the hypotenuse of 10.

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:20.340
The altitude to the hypotenuse is the vertical distance from this third point to the hypotenuse.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.140
This will vary, depending on which point it is on the circle.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.360
So we want to know what is the largest altitude we can make.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:32.520
Well, this will be when we move the altitude all the way to this point.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:33.520
 

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.560
This is the largest possible altitude of the right triangle,

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.600
and this is exactly equal to the radius of the circle.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.980
This is one half the diameter of 10,

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.840
which means the largest altitude you could have

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.260
is equal to 5.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.940
So the correct answer to this problem is there is a typo in the textbook.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:55.500
There is NO right triangle that has the hypotenuse equal to 10

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.340
and an altitude to the hypotenuse equal to 6.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:02.060
At most you can have an altitude equal to half of the hypotenuse

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:02.980
which would be 5.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:05.580
Did you figure it out?

